<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah [Laws of the Afternoon Prayer]<br>Halachah 7, Part 1 (§§ 1–16)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:49</span> (<i>LaShemesh Ohel Bahem</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">Introduction</div>
<div class="p">Based on the teaching "<span class="h">LaShemesh Ohel Bahem</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:49</span>). See there the entire teaching well.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — <i>Minchah</i>: Sweetening Judgments Through <i>Teshuvah</i> — Turning Bad Thoughts to Good</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>. Then is the essential sweetening of judgments — one must be careful to pray with great <span class="h">kavanah</span> to sweeten the fierce judgments aroused then (as the Zohar states, and as explained in the above teaching on the verse <span class="v">"fine flour for a <i>Minchah</i>"</span>). <span class="h">Minchah</span> is the aspect of judgments (= <span class="h">Pachad Yitzchak</span>; Yitzchak established <span class="h">Minchah</span>). The essential tikun and sweetening is through <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — returning from bad thoughts to good thoughts (for they are the essential <span class="h">yitzarim</span>). As explained there — <span class="h">tefilah</span>, <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, the ten types of melody, and the <span class="h">Hai</span> that was a <span class="h">Dales</span> are all connected. The essential thing: to overpower bad thoughts and transform them to good. The <span class="h">ohr lahaviyus</span> [fiery light] of a Jew's heart is the aspect of <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> — infinite, boundless yearning — unable to do any service or draw any quality. One must contract [<span class="h">tzamtzem</span>] this enthusiasm until there is a <span class="h">chalal</span> [hollow] in the heart: <span class="v">"my heart is hollow within me."</span> The heart is the axis of all qualities (= the worlds). The essential thing: the wisdom within the heart (= good thoughts). When one thinks bad thoughts, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, the hollow in the heart is blocked up. Therefore: contract the heart's enthusiasm — not too much (that being excessive light from which the Breaking of Vessels came). The enthusiasm must be gradual and measured: <span class="v">"my heart is hollow within me."</span> The essential: overpower bad thoughts with good ones — meriting all good qualities and serving Hashem properly. All to reveal His <span class="h">Malchus</span> — for this, everything was created.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — The Daily Cycle: <i>Arvis</i>, <i>Shacharis</i>, <i>Minchah</i>; the Poor Man's Prayer</div>

<div class="p">All this is <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>. Then the beginning of the contraction is aroused (from which all judgments derive) — and must be sweetened through focused prayer (= <span class="h">teshuvah</span>). The matter of the light's enthusiasm and contraction is replicated in man every day: just as at Creation, the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> had no place for creation until He contracted it — the wisdom emerged, producing all worlds — so too man (a portion of G-d from above): in his daily service, he must draw the tikun and sustenance of that day's creation anew. <span class="h">Arvis</span> [evening prayer] is the contraction of light (= darkness: <span class="v">"He placed darkness as His concealment"</span>). <span class="h">Shacharis</span> is the revelation and drawing of light through the tikun of the contraction — the <span class="h">mochin</span> are drawn (= that day's creation). But after prayer, the <span class="h">mochin</span> depart; only a <span class="h">reshimu</span> [trace] remains. After midday, as the day turns — the beginning of the contraction for the next day's creation stirs. Then <span class="mx">"harsh judgment prevails."</span> The Sages established <span class="h">Minchah</span> then, warning greatly: <span class="mx">"one should always be careful with <i>Tefilas HaMinchah</i>."</span></div>

<div class="p">Tremendous exertion is needed: nullifying the bad thoughts from the fierce judgments, drawing good thoughts. This is <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> of every person: returning each time from bad thoughts to holy thoughts. Thought is in man's hand at every moment — there lies the essential test and free will. Return in thought from wherever you are to holy thoughts. Even if one's thought strayed terribly — believe that every detachment from evil to good is precious to Hashem: <span class="mx">"He knows our inclination."</span> The essential evil inclination is the thoughts.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> is the poor man's offering (= <span class="h">tefilah l'ani</span>) — through daily business, one is rarely spared flawed thoughts (<span class="mx">"from three things no man is spared daily"</span>). <span class="h">Minchah</span> is <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: from amid all business, remembering Hashem — overpowering bad thoughts with good (= the tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span>: <span class="mx">"there is no poor man except in <i>da'as</i>"</span>). When one has mercy on oneself, sees one's poverty, does not despair, and prays — <span class="v">"a prayer for the poor man when he is wrapped [in his distress]"</span> — surrounded, unable to flee right or left, as David cried: <span class="v">"they encompassed me, they surrounded me"</span> — yet overpowers and cries to Hashem: this is <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>. Through this — the essential tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: the <span class="h">Hai</span> returns to its place. All judgments from the contraction are sweetened — revealed that within the contraction itself, Hashem is concealed. The contraction is a great kindness — to reveal His G-dliness and <span class="h">Malchus</span>. <span class="v">"Hashem, He is <i>Elokim</i>"</span> — <span class="h">Elokim</span> (contraction) revealed as Hashem: <span class="mx">"there is no place devoid of Him."</span> Said twice — for the ascent of both the upper house [<span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>] and the lower house [<span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>].</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — Eliyahu: "Answer Me" Twice — Fire from Heaven vs. Sorcery; the <i>Tzadik</i>'s Torah from Heaven</div>

<div class="p">Eliyahu said: <span class="v">"answer me, Hashem, answer me"</span> — our Sages: "answer me that fire descend" and "answer me that they not say it is sorcery." Eliyahu needed to reveal His G-dliness and <span class="h">Malchus</span> — saving Israel from the false prophets of the Ba'al (great sorcerers who brought Israel to ruin). Sorcery derives from the <span class="h">mosaros</span> [excess] from excessive light (as explained in "<span class="h">Bo el Pharaoh</span>"). Eliyahu needed to draw and reveal His light through fire descending from Heaven: <span class="v">"Hashem your G-d is a consuming fire"</span> — consuming and destroying all. But Israel, who contract the light and reveal His <span class="h">Malchus</span>, receive life from there: <span class="v">"you who cling to Hashem your G-d are alive, all of you, today."</span> Fire must descend in measure — revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>, destroying the false prophets. Israel fell on their faces, said <span class="v">"Hashem, He is <i>Elokim</i>"</span> twice — <span class="h">Malchus</span> revealed.</div>

<div class="p">But Eliyahu feared: they would say it was sorcery — that he was merely a greater sage who knew sorcery better than the Ba'al's prophets. He prayed: open their eyes and hearts to see the truth — a miracle from Heaven alone, through Hashem's will. So too in every generation: when a great <span class="h">tzadik</span> reveals wondrous <span class="h">chiddushim</span> impossible to refute (clearly Torah from Heaven, impossible by human intellect) — opponents first mock; when they see the <span class="h">chiddushim</span> cannot be dismissed, they say: "He was merely a great sage, capable of novel interpretations by intellect" — attributing them to human wisdom without true <span class="h">yirah</span>. This is "sorcery" — through cunning human intellect (given for free will). The true <span class="h">tzadik</span> always prays: "answer me — let fire descend" (= Torah from Heaven, which is fire: <span class="v">"His words we heard from amidst the fire"</span>) — "and answer me that they not say it is sorcery." May Hashem open their eyes and hearts to see that this is not human intellect at all — Torah from Heaven: a consuming fire destroying all who oppose it, but giving life forever to those who cling to it.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — The Two Hollows of the Heart; Ten Types of Melody; the Entire Sefer Tehillim</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Singers like those who hollow [<i>k'chalilim</i>] — all my wellsprings are in you"</span> — two hollows: the right hollow of the heart (dwelling of the <span class="h">yetzer tov</span>) and the left (dwelling of the <span class="h">yetzer hara</span>). Man's entire work: to empower the right over the left (= good thoughts over bad). Through good thoughts: <span class="v">"my heart is hollow within me"</span> — the tikun of the <span class="h">chalal hapanui</span> of Creation, where all worlds (= qualities) were drawn. Through bad thoughts: the <span class="h">chalal</span> is blocked — the Shechinah departs, returning to <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> (= Breaking of Vessels). The left overpowers the right — blocked heart: <span class="v">"foreskin of heart, disgrace of heart."</span> Through good thoughts: the <span class="h">chalal hapanui</span> is sustained. The <span class="h">tzadikim</span> maintain it: contracting their infinite enthusiasm properly, maintaining <span class="v">"my heart is hollow"</span>, and overpowering the bad thoughts that seek to block the heart (whose success would withdraw the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> — destruction of the world). Through good thoughts: all qualities (= sustenance of all worlds) are drawn; His <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed to all. Then the <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> also ascends: <span class="v">"I shall not enter the city"</span> — only after <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed does the light of the heart return in tikun: one yearns for Hashem with wondrous, awesome longing — nearly infinite — yet contracted: <span class="v">"my heart is hollow."</span> = <span class="v">"my soul thirsts for G-d"</span>; <span class="v">"my heart and flesh sing to the Living G-d."</span> This is the entire Sefer Tehillim of David (the pleasant songster of Israel) — all the ten types of melody: yearning, longing, desire reaching beyond limit — yet existing (ascending and descending = the art of melody). Therefore Rabbainu connected the ten types of melody in this teaching.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — "Whoever Makes Himself Small Is Great"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="mx">"Whoever makes himself small is great; whoever makes himself great is small."</span> Whoever diminishes himself (= contracting the light = <span class="v">"Moshe hid his face"</span> = <span class="v">"for you are the fewest"</span> — "you diminish yourselves when I bestow good upon you"): even when receiving true good (= the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span>'s flash), he diminishes himself (= contracts the light). Then he merits wondrous yearning — <span class="mx">"the small one is great."</span> Conversely, whoever is "great" and seeks to break through, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — he becomes small (the light departs entirely).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — <i>Na'aseh</i> Before <i>Nishma</i>: Action Before Perception</div>

<div class="p">Israel's greatness: they said <span class="v">"we shall do"</span> [<span class="h">na'aseh</span>] before <span class="v">"we shall hear"</span> [<span class="h">nishma</span>]. <span class="h">Na'aseh</span> = service, practical <span class="h">mitzvos</span> (= drawing good qualities, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> in the world: <span class="h">Malchus</span> = <span class="h">Asiyah</span>). <span class="h">Nishma</span> = d'vaikus and yearning (hearing depends on the heart) — only after <span class="h">na'aseh</span> (= tikun of <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>) comes the tikun of <span class="h">nishma</span> (= ascent of <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>). The nations are the opposite: wanting to perceive everything first (= <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>), then to act — but Hashem rejected this, for He desires that man overcome through free choice (= contracting the light). The concealment of knowledge is the contraction of light at Creation's beginning. Man must do likewise: contract excessive enthusiasm, overpower bad thoughts — then he will perceive what he perceives. All supreme perceptions come through tikun of the <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> (= apprehending <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 7 — The <i>Tzadik</i> Draws Power from <i>K'nesses Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">tzadik</span> must receive all his power from <span class="h">K'nesses Yisrael</span> [the collective of Israel] (= <span class="h">Malchus</span>: <span class="mx">"there is no king without a nation"</span>). Upon them His <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed; then the <span class="h">tzadik</span> strengthens in his awesome perceptions (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> = <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span>). The essential thing: Israel must leave the external wisdoms and bad thoughts — then the <span class="h">tzadik</span> <span class="v">"rejoices like a mighty man running the course"</span> that Israel can walk on when in disgrace. This is the essential tikun of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> for everyone — impossible without the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who shows the path for those returning.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 8 — The <i>Ba'al Teshuvah</i>'s Flame: Do Not Let Excessive Enthusiasm Prevent <i>Teshuvah</i></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">ba'al teshuvah</span> needs this especially — having already blocked his heart through foolishness. When a thought of <span class="h">teshuvah</span> stirs (from the concealed spark [<span class="h">nekudah</span>] remaining in the heart for his survival), the spark blazes and seeks to cling to its root in the <span class="h">Ohr Ain Sof</span> — wanting immediately to take upon himself all the <span class="h">teshuvos</span> in the world (fasts, mortifications beyond measure, knowing how much he damaged). But even though this is fitting, he should not let it prevent <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: he immediately retreats, thinking he cannot fulfill such stringencies — and abandons <span class="h">teshuvah</span> entirely. This is exceedingly common. Israel are a holy people — all desire to return, but most are prevented by excessive enthusiasm (through which they are completely extinguished, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore: the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is through the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and their students who received true paths from their master. In truth, <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is when a person returns to the places where he was originally and turns from them (as heard from Rabbainu's holy mouth). The essential thing depends on thought: when one overpowers bad thoughts — even if they intensely overpower — each moment of resistance is <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: <span class="v">"at every moment You test him."</span> With perseverance, one can eventually merit complete <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. No good will [<span class="h">ra'usa tava</span>] is ever lost. This cannot all be written — for this one must travel to the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and their students: <span class="mx">"make for yourself a Rav and acquire a friend"</span> — receiving true counsel from very deep waters. Through this alone there is hope. The essential tikun for everyone: Hashem is exceedingly great and we know nothing at all. We must only nullify our understanding before the <span class="h">tzadik</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 9 — <i>T'chailes</i> That Consumes; Israel Receives Life by Clinging</div>

<div class="p">As explained in the teaching (§7): <span class="h">t'chailes</span> [the blue thread] <span class="mx">"consumes and destroys all"</span> — but Israel who cling to Hashem receive life from there. The nations, not overpowering bad thoughts and pursuing external wisdoms (from the Breaking of Vessels through excessive light — wanting to know everything without breaking a single bad quality), are consumed and burned (= excessive light). But Israel — clinging through the above tikun, contracting, overpowering bad thoughts, revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> — receive all their life from there. The <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>) receives power from <span class="h">K'nesses Yisrael</span> (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>). Both aspects exist in general and particular. Even after the <span class="h">tzadik</span> has already achieved tikun of both houses — in the totality of the worlds, he needs power from all Israel: <span class="mx">"did I give you greatness except for their sake?"</span> Therefore the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> labor enormously to bring Israel to <span class="h">teshuvah</span> (especially the most debased) — the lower <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed, the greater the ascent. <span class="h">Malchus</span> nests in <span class="h">Asiyah</span> (Zohar) — His essential will is to reveal <span class="h">Malchus</span> in the deepest depths of <span class="h">Asiyah</span>. Then: <span class="v">"everything that has breath praises Hashem"</span>; <span class="v">"His <i>Malchus</i> rules over all"</span> — the <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> ascends, and the <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascends as well. The more distant souls are returned, the greater the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s ascent — as the teaching concludes: <span class="v">"for the sun He made a tent within them"</span> — the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s radiance (= perception) only through Israel.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 10 — <i>Parshas Shekalim</i>; the <i>Mishkan</i>; the Golden Calf Through Excessive Light</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Parshas Shekalim</span>: <span class="v">"half a <i>shekel</i>, an offering to Hashem"</span> — the enthusiasm must be weighed and measured. All for building the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> (= the Bais HaMikdash = <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>): through it, <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> is also repaired (<span class="v">"I shall not enter the city above until..."</span>). The <span class="h">Mishkan</span> was <span class="h">teshuvah</span> for the sin of the Golden Calf (which came through excessive light — Rashi: "the first <span class="h">Luchos</span>, given with fanfare, were afflicted by the evil eye — nothing is finer than modesty"). Despite being warned (<span class="v">"let them not break through"</span>), the light exceeded their capacity: <span class="v">"the nobles of Israel... gazed at G-d, ate and drank"</span> — Rashi: they were deserving of punishment, gazing from a coarse heart. The masses (especially the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span>) damaged in the physical dimension — bad thoughts: <span class="mx">"Israel did not worship idolatry except to permit themselves forbidden relations."</span> All interconnected: excessive enthusiasm → bad thoughts overpowering → consuming fire from cravings: <span class="v">"they ascend to heaven, descend to depths."</span></div>

<div class="p">After the Calf, Moshe was commanded to build the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> — <span class="h">teshuvah</span> for the sin encompassing all sins. Through the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (= Moshe): contract enthusiasm, each according to his level; overpower bad thoughts (the essential evil inclination). Thought is always in man's hand — he must redirect: flee bad thoughts, think a verse, a <span class="h">halachah</span>, a teaching; sometimes even mundane thoughts — anything to rescue from evil thoughts. Even if deeply trapped (common in many people) — persist with every strategy; believe every movement of detachment is exceedingly precious to Hashem. Enter good thoughts, flee to the book, study Torah — until meriting the tikun of qualities (= all <span class="h">mitzvos</span>) in the heart (= tikun of the worlds), revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span>. <span class="mx">"Every person must say: the world was created for me"</span> — even the least of Israel was created to have a part in revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span>. This = tikun of <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>; then <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> also ascends. Yet one must always be careful to contract enthusiasm and guard intensely against bad thoughts: <span class="v">"the wicked one watches the <i>tzadik</i>"</span>; <span class="v">"he lurks in hiding like a lion, lurks to snatch the poor man."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 11 — The <i>Mishkan</i> in the Wilderness: Every Good Point Stored Safely</div>

<div class="p">Moshe built the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> in the wilderness. The essential tikun is through the Bais HaMikdash — the holy place designated for revealing <span class="h">Malchus</span> to all. Moshe yearned to enter Eretz Yisrael. Without the sins of the Calf and the spies, they would have entered immediately (our Sages say). But through great corruption they were delayed. Hashem had mercy — commanding the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> in the wilderness with such holiness that even there (among <span class="v">"fiery serpent, scorpion, and thirst"</span>) the Bais HaMikdash's holiness could be drawn.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Mishkan</span>'s essential holiness: whoever has a share in building it through generous-hearted donation (body, money, desire) — every <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, every good point snatched from amid all confusion and obstacles and worldly fire — all can be brought into this holy place (= the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> = the Bais HaMikdash = the <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span>). There all Israel gather; there all good points of all Israel's holiness converge. Through this, each person has the strength to remain in Israel's holiness, strengthening himself each time to return from bad thoughts — <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. Even when overpowered terribly — the good snatched before is already entered and stored in the exceedingly holy place of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>, combined with all Israel's good points gathered there. No <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> can damage this tikun. All ascends to Hashem. Through this, one can begin anew all one's life — until returning to Hashem: <span class="v">"Hashem, You have been our dwelling in every generation"</span> (= the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> and Bais HaMikdash) — <span class="v">"You return man to <i>dakah</i> [being crushed]"</span>: anyone can return to Hashem even from the crushing of soul, physically and spiritually.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 12 — <i>Batei K'nesiyos</i> and <i>Batei Midrashos</i>: The <i>Mishkan</i> in Exile; <i>Parshas Shekalim</i></div>

<div class="p">Even now in our bitter exile, all our life and sustenance is through <span class="h">batei k'nesiyos</span> [synagogues] and <span class="h">batei midrashos</span> [study halls] (= the holiness of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>: <span class="mx">"a miniature sanctuary"</span>). Moshe, seeing the Adversary's fierce overpowering (after Sinai's great revelation — still they sinned so terribly), created a tikun for all generations: the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> in the wilderness. Even after both Temples' destruction — we can still draw the Bais HaMikdash's holiness even in <span class="h">Chutz LaAretz</span>, in the distant northern lands, through <span class="h">batei k'nesiyos</span> and <span class="h">batei midrashos</span>. Therefore <span class="h">Mishkan</span> = <span class="h">Hamshachah</span> [drawing]: through the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>'s holiness in the wilderness — and through reading its <span class="h">parshiyos</span> in Torah — we draw the Bais HaMikdash's holiness into exile.</div>

<div class="p">The essential power comes through Moshe (= the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>). We must come to them, joining their <span class="h">kibbutz</span> [gathering] in <span class="h">batei midrashos</span> built in their name. They, in their great power, engage even now in building the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> (as explained in "<span class="h">Azamrah</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:282</span>: every true <span class="h">tzadik</span> builds a <span class="h">Mishkan</span>). There, each person can store every good point — entering it into the holy <span class="h">kibbutz</span>'s partnership where no destroyer can reach it. <span class="h">Bereishis</span> = <span class="h">Rosh Bayis</span> [head of the house] — <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = <span class="v">"<i>Bereishis</i> — <i>yirah Shabbos</i>"</span> [awe of Shabbos]. The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is through the Bais Midrash the <span class="h">tzadik</span> builds (= the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>). Through this one merits <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika S'tima'ah</span> (= <span class="h">oneg Shabbos</span>).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 13 — <i>Shekalim</i> as <i>Tzedakah</i> and <i>Teshuvah</i>: Tikun of the <i>Hai</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Shekalim</span> are <span class="h">tzedakah</span> — especially <span class="h">tzedakah</span> for building the Bais Midrash (the most enduring <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, as Rashi explains on <span class="v">"whoever loves abundance, no income"</span>). <span class="h">Tzedakah</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span>: drawing the <span class="h">Hai</span> from <span class="h">Tzadik</span> to <span class="h">Tzedakah</span>. The <span class="h">Hai</span> was a <span class="h">Dales</span> — all poverty and want of the poor derives from the fierce contraction's judgment. Giving <span class="h">tzedakah</span> draws the <span class="h">Yud</span> into the <span class="h">Hai</span> — raising the poor from poverty: <span class="v">"a tenth of an <i>aifah</i> of fine flour for a <i>Minchah</i>."</span> Drawing holiness through holy thoughts and the abundance of holy wealth into the <span class="h">Dales</span> that was poor — making it a <span class="h">Hai</span>: all one concept. <span class="h">Tzedakah</span> to the worthy poor (= the <span class="h">Shechinah</span>: <span class="v">"I dwell with the crushed"</span>; <span class="v">"let not the crushed one return in disgrace"</span>) — through giving, the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> is raised from poverty: the <span class="h">Hai</span> is restored. <span class="h">Tzedakah</span> = tikun of the <span class="h">Hai</span> = the above tikun = tikun of the <span class="h">Shekalim</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 14 — The Census: Contracting Each Individual; "Standing Crowded, Bowing with Space"</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"When you take the count of the Children of Israel... each shall give a ransom — that there be no plague."</span> Why count them with risk of plague — why not simply not count? The census was necessary: counting = contraction. Each Jew's fiery light is <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> — where there is no number at all: <span class="mx">"before the One, what do you count?"</span> Number begins only after the contraction. Each must be counted individually (= contracting each one's light to its own boundary). Each person has his own boundary and measure in knowing Hashem — <span class="v">"known at the gates is her husband — each according to what he measures in his heart."</span> Each serves through his own qualities: one is careful with this <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, another with that (= different qualities): <span class="mx">"your father — in which was he most careful?"</span></div>

<div class="p">One should receive from each person's unique good point — yet must not break through another's boundary, nor let enthusiasm cause one to trespass. Counting = each numbered separately. In the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> and Bais HaMikdash: all Israel gather as one — yet each stands on his boundary: <span class="mx">"standing crowded yet bowing with space"</span> — pressed together as one, yet each bowing privately: <span class="v">"one should not hear his fellow's confession."</span> Each returns to Hashem according to his level. This tikun is achieved in the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> and Bais HaMikdash. Therefore they were counted through <span class="h">shekalim</span> donated to the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>: light drawn in measure and weight to each according to his level. The <span class="h">shekalim</span> are named for <span class="h">mishkal</span> [weight/measure].</div>

<div class="sn">§ 15 — The <i>Erev Rav</i>: Mixing That Which Is Forbidden to Investigate in Public</div>

<div class="p">All corruption was through the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> (= <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> — a great mixture): mixing their knowledge in improper combinations, seeking to investigate that which is forbidden (<span class="mx">"do not inquire into that which is beyond you"</span>). Even in what is permitted to contemplate, certain matters are forbidden publicly: <span class="mx">"do not expound forbidden relations with three, nor the Act of Creation with two, nor the Chariot alone — unless he is a sage who understands on his own."</span> The wicked and deniers who seek to grasp Him through philosophical proofs do the opposite — entering forbidden investigations, teaching publicly in their evil assemblies. This is the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> — mixing and confusing everything (through them the Calf's sin — idolatry and denial). The tikun: <span class="h">shekalim</span> for the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>'s donation and <span class="h">tzedakah</span> to Torah scholars. Drawing the abundance of wealth to holiness (opposite the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> who used abundant wealth for the Calf: <span class="v">"and much gold"</span>).</div>

<div class="p"><i>[Heads of chapters connected to Hilchos Minchah — the matter of Purim:]</i></div>

<div class="sn">§ 16 — Purim, Pesach, and the <i>Mishkan</i>: <i>Baisa Ila'ah</i> and <i>Baisa Tata'ah</i></div>

<div class="p">Purim is the preparation for Pesach — subduing Haman-Amalek. The Ari"zal writes: Haman-Amalek's essential provocation was because he saw the exile was ending and Israel wished to build the Bais HaMikdash. The essential tikun of everything is through the Bais HaMikdash. The principle: <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> = <span class="h">Binah</span> and <span class="h">Malchus</span> = heart and mouth. What one knows of Hashem through the heart's contemplation (each according to what he measures in his heart) = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> = perception of G-dliness through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'aila</span> [arousal from above] — the soul's nature to burn for Hashem infinitely. But this is not the completeness: one must contract the enthusiasm, serve with measure and gradation (= creating worlds = qualities). The essential: through wisdom in the heart (good thoughts overpowering bad), revealing His <span class="h">Malchus</span> for all to see — expressed in <span class="h">tefilah</span> through the mouth specifically, binding thought-in-the-heart to speech with a firm, strong bond. Through this: <span class="h">Malchus</span> revealed = holy <span class="h">emunah</span>: <span class="v">"I shall make known Your <i>emunah</i> with my mouth."</span> <span class="h">Baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> are encompassed and both ascend. <span class="h">Avodah shebalev</span> [service of the heart] = <span class="h">tefilah</span>. The essential tikun is through <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> [arousal from below] — when <span class="h">Malchus</span> is revealed below, <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascends.</div>

<div class="p">The totality of worlds is called <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'aila</span> (= <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>), and <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> (= <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>). Above as well, in every world from <span class="h">Atzilus</span> to <span class="h">Asiyah</span>, both houses exist. So too in every person: <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> and <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span> = heart and mouth. All depends on man (= free will). Every flash of G-dliness illuminating man's heart from above = <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span>. When he exerts and reveals <span class="h">Malchus</span> = <span class="h">baisa tata'ah</span>. Then <span class="h">baisa ila'ah</span> ascends — receiving the flash of G-dliness in the heart.</div>

